  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nUpper ridging over the western and central portions of the U.S.\nextends into eastern Montana Monday and Tuesday. Given NE Montana\nwill be on the northeast quadrant of the ridge under northwest\nflow aloft, weak embedded shortwaves may be enough to set off a\nfew thunderstorms across the northeast zones Monday and/or Tuesday\nevening. It should be a fairly isolated but potentially strong\nstorm via high res model guidance, so will keep patches of slight\nchance or silent pops in place.\n\nAs an aside, wind speeds may marginally support Lake Wind criteria\nfor Fort Peck Lake this afternoon, especially near the dam. These\nbreezy conditions are expected to be small in coverage and short\nin duration so have opted not to issue an advisory at this time.\n\nFor Wednesday into Friday, upper ridging amplifies as the ridge\naxis pushes across central Montana and adjacent Canada with a\nwarming trend resuming. Conditions will largely be dry save for an\nisolated thunderstorm with the diurnal heating and instability,\nand weak embedded disturbances. Most places however will remain\ndry this period.\n\nThe ridge axis will begin to slide downstream on Friday into the\nweekend, lending to a return to triple digit heat across the area\nas well as a continuation of thunderstorm chances at times. If\nthe current ensemble means hold, eventual heat related products\nmay offer utility.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nEnsemble consensus is fairly solid depicting upper ridging over\nthe foreseeable future. This lends to above average confidence for\nhot and mostly dry weather through the week. Confidence is below\naverage for specific timing and placement of thunderstorms\nthrough the next couple of days due to limited expected coverage.\nThat said, its evolution is depicted in updraft helicity tracks\namong the high resolution model suite. Confidence is lower on\nconvection during the mid to end of the week due to the later time\nscales.",
